EffectCraft + OpenFX @LABEL@ (macOS @ARCH@, unofficial)
==========================================================

This is a build of EffectCraft that can run OpenFX plug-ins and open After Effects projects.
It is NOT made by the EffectCraft maintainers; report problems to
https://github.com/glumbis/effectcraft/issues

1. Install
----------
Drag EffectCraft.app into your Applications folder.

2. Open it the first time (the app is not notarized)
----------------------------------------------------
The app is signed only with a local ad-hoc signature, because this project has no Apple developer
certificate. macOS therefore blocks it the first time ("EffectCraft can't be opened because Apple
cannot check it for malicious software"). Either:

  a) Open Terminal and run, once:

         xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/EffectCraft.app

     (also run it for this folder if you want to use "Set up After Effects import.command":
      xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine "<this folder>")

  or

  b) Try to open EffectCraft, then go to  System Settings > Privacy & Security, scroll down to
     "EffectCraft was blocked" and click "Open Anyway". (On macOS 14 and older you can also
     right-click the app and choose Open.)

You only need to do this once per download.

3. OpenFX plug-ins
------------------
Install your plug-ins with their own installers, choosing the OFX version. They go into
/Library/OFX/Plugins, where EffectCraft finds them automatically. A tiny test plug-in ("EC Test" in
Effects & Presets) is already inside the app. If you cannot see your plug-ins, look at the report
EffectCraft writes at start-up: ~/Library/Application Support/EffectCraft/ofx_scan.txt

To add a plug-in by hand without an administrator password, copy its .ofx.bundle folder into
~/Library/OFX/Plugins (create the folder if it is not there) or into
~/Library/Application Support/EffectCraft/OFX. EffectCraft searches both, and neither is touched when
you replace or delete the app.

Only macOS builds of plug-ins work (a "MacOS" folder inside the .ofx.bundle), and they must match
this app's processor: this download is for @ARCH@ Macs.

4. After Effects project import (.aep / .aepx)
----------------------------------------------
Needs Python 3 and the py-aep library. Double-click "Set up After Effects import.command" (run the
xattr command from step 2 on this folder first). It keeps py-aep in a private Python environment,
~/Library/Application Support/EffectCraft/aep-python, which EffectCraft finds by itself (a plain
"pip install --user" is refused by recent Pythons). Or do the same in Terminal:

    /usr/bin/python3 -m venv "$HOME/Library/Application Support/EffectCraft/aep-python"
    "$HOME/Library/Application Support/EffectCraft/aep-python/bin/pip" install py-aep

(Homebrew's or python.org's Python 3 work too: /opt/homebrew/bin/python3, /usr/local/bin/python3.)
Then use File > Import > After Effects Project... in EffectCraft.

5. Command line
---------------
/Applications/EffectCraft.app/Contents/MacOS/effectcraft-cli --help

Requires macOS 11 (Big Sur) or newer. More in README.md.
